## 🧬 Tier 4: Client-Side Object Modification (Nested Property Injection)
When you type an amount into the withdrawal field, the front-end application wraps that number into a data object. Scanners only fuzz the obvious amount key. You look for the hidden parameters developers pass silently alongside it.
## 1. Overriding the Limit Properties
Intercept the withdrawal request and look closely at the full JSON payload structure. Often, developers pass metadata flags that define the limits dynamically to save database queries.

* The Payload: Look for parameters like this:

{
  "amount": 5500,
  "currency": "USD"
}

Manually inject properties designed to overwrite the backend model validation logic:

{
  "amount": 5500,
  "currency": "USD",
  "bypass_limits": true,
  "min_limit": 0,
  "max_limit": 100000,
  "ignore_constraints": 1
}

* The Hidden Logic: If the backend database code uses an open object-mapping framework (Mass Assignment) to initialize the validation class, passing these custom fields can overwrite the internal limit boundaries for your session, letting you process an amount that would normally be blocked.

